Lab 19: Troubleshooting Multi-Cluster Management Issues
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Analyze RHACM logs to identify and diagnose multi-cluster management issues
Utilize metrics and CLI tools to troubleshoot cluster health problems
Apply systematic debugging techniques to resolve application deployment failures
Detect and remediate governance policy violations
Perform root cause analysis for cluster management failures using scripted diagnostic tools
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes concepts and kubectl/oc commands
Familiarity with Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) fundamentals
Knowledge of YAML configuration files and Linux command line
Understanding of container orchestration principles
Experience with basic troubleshooting methodologies
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines with all necessary tools installed. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment - no need to build your own VM or install software.

Your lab environment includes:

RHACM Hub cluster pre-installed
Two managed clusters (cluster1 and cluster2)
Pre-configured monitoring and logging tools
Sample applications and policies for troubleshooting scenarios
The oc CLI already authenticated against the hub cluster
No additional package installation is required. All scripts in this lab use only bash, oc, curl, and jq, which are preinstalled in the environment. If jq is missing for any reason, install it with:

sudo yum install -y jq || sudo apt-get install -y jq
Task 1: Using RHACM Logs and Metrics for Troubleshooting
Subtask 1.1: Accessing and Analyzing Hub Cluster Logs
Step 1: Connect to your lab environment and verify RHACM installation

# Check RHACM operator status
oc get pods -n open-cluster-management

# Verify hub cluster status
oc get multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management
Expected output: a list of running pods (e.g., multicluster-operators-hub, cluster-manager, search-collector) and a MultiClusterHub resource reporting STATUS: Running.

Step 2: Create a working directory and a log collection script

mkdir -p ~/rhacm-troubleshooting
cd ~/rhacm-troubleshooting

cat > collect-rhacm-logs.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== RHACM Log Collection Script ==="
LOG_DIR="rhacm-logs-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"
