Task 3: Troubleshooting Deployment Failures and Policy Violations
Subtask 3.1: Diagnosing and Fixing a Broken Application Deployment
Step 1: Create the application deployment troubleshooter script

cat > app-deployment-troubleshooter.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

APP_NAME=${1:-"sample-app"}
NAMESPACE=${2:-"default"}

echo "=== Application Deployment Troubleshooter ==="
echo "Application: $APP_NAME"
echo "Namespace: $NAMESPACE"

echo "=== Application Status ==="
oc get application "$APP_NAME" -n "$NAMESPACE" -o yaml 2>/dev/null || echo "Application not found"

echo "=== Subscription Status ==="
SUBSCRIPTIONS=$(oc get subscription -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app="$APP_NAME" --no-headers 2>/dev/null)
if [ -n "$SUBSCRIPTIONS" ]; then
    oc get subscription -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app="$APP_NAME" -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PHASE:.status.phase,MESSAGE:.status.message
else
    echo "No subscriptions found for application $APP_NAME"
fi

echo "=== Placement Rules ==="
oc get placementrule -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app="$APP_NAME" -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,SATISFIED:.status.satisfied 2>/dev/null

echo "=== Placement Decisions ==="
oc get placementdecision -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app="$APP_NAME" 2>/dev/null

echo "=== Deployed Resources on Target Clusters ==="
for cluster in $(oc get managedclusters -o name | cut -d'/' -f2); do
    echo "Checking cluster: $cluster"
    oc get manifestwork -n "$cluster" 2>/dev/null | grep "$APP_NAME" || echo "No manifestwork found on $cluster"
done

echo "=== Common Issues Check ==="
echo "1. Image pull / mount errors:"
oc get events -n "$NAMESPACE" --field-selector reason=Failed 2>/dev/null | grep -i "image\|pull" || echo "None found"

echo "2. Scheduling failures:"
oc get events -n "$NAMESPACE" --field-selector reason=FailedScheduling 2>/dev/null || echo "None found"

echo "3. RBAC issues:"
oc get events -n "$NAMESPACE" --field-selector reason=FailedCreate 2>/dev/null | grep -i "forbidden\|unauthorized" || echo "None found"
EOF

chmod +x app-deployment-troubleshooter.sh
Step 2: Deploy a sample application with an intentional placement misconfiguration, then run the troubleshooter against it

cat > problematic-app.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: app.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: problematic-app
  namespace: default
spec:
  componentKinds:
  - group: apps.open-cluster-management.io
    kind: Subscription
  descriptor: {}
  selector:
    matchExpressions:
    - key: app
      operator: In
      values:
      - problematic-app
---
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: problematic-app-subscription
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: problematic-app
spec:
  channel: default/sample-channel
  placement:
    placementRef:
      name: problematic-app-placement
      kind: PlacementRule
---
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: problematic-app-placement
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: problematic-app
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    matchLabels:
      environment: nonexistent  # No managed cluster has this label - causes placement failure
EOF

oc apply -f problematic-app.yaml

echo "Testing troubleshooter..."
./app-deployment-troubleshooter.sh problematic-app default
Expected output: the Application and Subscription resources are found, but the PlacementRule shows SATISFIED: False (or no PlacementDecision is created at all), and the "Deployed Resources on Target Clusters" section reports "No manifestwork found" on both cluster1 and cluster2 â�� confirming the application never actually shipped anywhere due to the bad clusterSelector.

Step 3: Fix the placement issue and re-verify

cat > fixed-placement.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: problematic-app-placement
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: problematic-app
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    matchLabels: {}
EOF

oc apply -f fixed-placement.yaml

echo "Re-testing troubleshooter after fix..."
sleep 15
./app-deployment-troubleshooter.sh problematic-app default
Deliverable: after re-applying the corrected PlacementRule (using an empty selector to match any available managed cluster), the troubleshooter output should now show SATISFIED: True for the placement rule and at least one manifestwork entry listed under cluster1 and/or cluster2.

Subtask 3.2: Detecting and Resolving Policy Violations
Step 1: Create the policy violation detector and apply a sample policy with an intentional violation

cat > policy-violation-detector.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Policy Violation Detector ==="

echo "=== Policy Compliance Overview ==="
oc get policy -A -o custom-columns=NAMESPACE:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,COMPLIANCE:.status.compliant 2>/dev/null

echo "=== Non-Compliant Policies ==="
NON_COMPLIANT=$(oc get policy -A --no-headers 2>/dev/null | grep -v "Compliant$")
if [ -n "$NON_COMPLIANT" ]; then
    echo "$NON_COMPLIANT"
else
    echo "All policies report Compliant (re-run after applying the sample policy below)"
fi

echo "=== Configuration Policy Templates ==="
oc get configurationpolicy -A -o custom-columns=NAMESPACE:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,COMPLIANCE:.status.compliant 2>/dev/null

echo "=== Placement Bindings ==="
oc get placementbinding -A -o custom-columns=NAMESPACE:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,POLICY:.subjects[0].name,PLACEMENT:.placementRef.name 2>/dev/null

echo "=== Recent Policy Events ==="
oc get events -A --field-selector reason=PolicyStatusSync 2>/dev/null --sort-by='.lastTimestamp' | tail -10
EOF

chmod +x policy-violation-detector.sh

cat > sample-security-policy.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: Policy
metadata:
  name: security-policy
  namespace: default
spec:
  remediationAction: inform
  disabled: false
  policy-templates:
  - objectDefinition:
      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1
      kind: ConfigurationPolicy
      metadata:
        name: security-policy-config
      spec:
        remediationAction: inform
        severity: high
        object-templates:
        - complianceType: mustnothave
          objectDefinition:
            apiVersion: v1
            kind: Pod
            metadata:
              namespace: default
            spec:
              containers:
              - name: test
                securityContext:
                  privileged: true
---
apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementBinding
metadata:
  name: security-policy-binding
  namespace: default
placementRef:
  name: security-policy-placement
  kind: PlacementRule
  apiGroup: apps.open-cluster-management.io
subjects:
- name: security-policy
  kind: Policy
  apiGroup: policy.open-cluster-management.io
---
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: security-policy-placement
  namespace: default
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    matchLabels: {}
EOF

oc apply -f sample-security-policy.yaml

cat > privileged-pod.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: privileged-test-pod
  namespace: default
spec:
  containers:
  - name: test
    image: nginx
    securityContext:
      privileged: true
EOF

oc apply -f privileged-pod.yaml

echo "Waiting for policy evaluation..."
sleep 20
./policy-violation-detector.sh
Expected output: the security-policy and its nested security-policy-config configuration policy should now show COMPLIANCE: NonCompliant, since the privileged-test-pod violates the mustnothave rule against privileged: true containers.

Step 2: Create the policy violation resolver and remediate the violation

cat > policy-violation-resolver.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

POLICY_NAME=${1:-"security-policy"}
NAMESPACE=${2:-"default"}

echo "=== Policy Violation Resolver ==="
echo "Policy: $POLICY_NAME"
echo "Namespace: $NAMESPACE"

echo "=== Policy Details ==="
oc get policy "$POLICY_NAME" -n "$NAMESPACE" -o yaml | grep -A 20 "status:"

analyze_violations() {
    echo "=== Violation Analysis ==="
    CONFIG_POLICIES=$(oc get configurationpolicy -A --no-headers 2>/dev/null | grep "$POLICY_NAME" | awk '{print $2}')
    for config_policy in $CONFIG_POLICIES; do
        echo "Analyzing configuration policy: $config_policy"
        oc get configurationpolicy "$config_policy" -n "$NAMESPACE" -o jsonpath='{.status.compliancyDetails[*].conditions[*].message}' 2>/dev/null | tr ' ' '\n'
    done
}

remediate_violations() {
    echo "=== Remediation Options ==="
    REMEDIATION_ACTION=$(oc get policy "$POLICY_NAME" -n "$NAMESPACE" -o jsonpath='{.spec.remediationAction}')
    echo "Current remediation action: $REMEDIATION_ACTION"

    if [ "$REMEDIATION_ACTION" = "inform" ]; then
        echo "Policy is in inform mode. Removing the violating resource directly, since this policy type only informs:"
        oc delete pod privileged-test-pod -n "$NAMESPACE" --ignore-not-found=true
    fi

    echo "Remaining privileged pods (should be empty):"
    oc get pods -n "$NAMESPACE" -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.containers[*].securityContext.privileged}{"\n"}{end}' | grep true || echo "None found"
}

create_compliant_resources() {
    echo "=== Creating Compliant Alternative ==="
    cat > compliant-pod.yaml << 'EOYAML'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: compliant-test-pod
  namespace: default
spec:
  containers:
  - name: test
    image: nginx
    securityContext:
      privileged: false
      runAsNonRoot: true
      runAsUser: 1000
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      capabilities:
        drop:
        - ALL
EOYAML
    oc apply -f compliant-pod.yaml
    echo "Compliant pod applied: compliant-test-pod"
}

analyze_violations
remediate_violations
create_compliant_resources

echo "=== Final Policy Status (allow a few seconds for re-evaluation) ==="
sleep 15
oc get policy "$POLICY_NAME" -n "$NAMESPACE" -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,COMPLIANCE:.status.compliant
EOF

chmod +x policy-violation-resolver.sh
./policy-violation-resolver.sh security-policy default
Deliverable: the final line of output shows security-policy with COMPLIANCE: Compliant, confirming the violation was analyzed, the offending pod removed, and a compliant replacement pod (compliant-test-pod) deployed in its place.

Task 3 Checkpoint: Confirm three concrete artifacts exist: (1) problematic-app with a satisfied placement and at least one manifestwork on a managed cluster, (2) security-policy reporting Compliant, and (3) a running compliant-test-pod in the default namespace with no privileged containers remaining.

