Conclusion
In this lab, students built a complete diagnostic toolkit for troubleshooting Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management deployments, progressing from log and metrics collection on the hub cluster, through cluster health and resource-constraint analysis on managed clusters, to hands-on resolution of a broken application placement and a governance policy violation. Along the way, students confirmed each fix with concrete, checkable evidence: a satisfied placement rule with a manifestwork on a target cluster, and a policy transitioning from NonCompliant to Compliant after remediation. These exercises reinforced the core troubleshooting methodology used in production RHACM environments â�� inspect logs and events, isolate the failing component (placement, subscription, agent connectivity, or policy template), apply a targeted fix, and verify the result. Students are encouraged to extend this toolkit by adding checks for additional RHACM addons (such as cert-policy-controller or iam-policy-controller) and by consulting the official Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management documentation for deeper coverage of observability and governance troubleshooting workflows.
